"""Longer, topic-specific evidence and case development for graded reading.
These are authored continuations, not length-padding templates.
"""
EXTENSIONS={}
def extend(week, first, second): EXTENSIONS[week]=(first,second)

extend(21,
'Making the confirmation useful~The confirmation message included an arrival time, the name of the shift leader and a short description of the role. Volunteers could reply if they needed a change, but they were asked not to swap duties privately without informing the coordinator. This was particularly important for jobs involving the cold store, where specific training was required. A simple swap between friends could otherwise leave nobody qualified for a necessary task.||Listening to people who left~The coordinator also contacted several volunteers who had stopped attending. One explained that bus fares made short shifts expensive; another had found the original role physically difficult. Neither problem appeared in the cancellation figures because those people had already disappeared from the active list. The next review therefore considered minimum shift length and less demanding roles. Improving the rota meant learning from absent volunteers as well as rewarding the reliability of those who remained.',
'Giving instructions without blame~The written instructions used photographs of correctly packed parcels and a separate example of a package that needed checking. They did not display photographs of volunteers making mistakes. This helped keep the emphasis on the task rather than on embarrassment. When a new volunteer was unsure, the shift leader asked them to explain what they had noticed. Their uncertainty sometimes revealed a missing instruction that experienced staff had stopped noticing.||A boundary for personal help~At the end of one shift, a visitor asked whether I could deliver food to a relative on my way home. I wanted to help, but I did not know the charity\'s delivery rules. I referred the request to the coordinator instead of making a personal promise. She checked consent, the address and the available service. I learned that being helpful includes knowing when a request needs someone with the right responsibility, rather than agreeing immediately to everything.')

extend(22,
'A record that explains the work~One worker pointed out that damaged labels were a common source of difficulty. The original training examples all used clean, easily readable barcodes. The trainer added a damaged-label exercise and showed how to request a replacement code. Staff were specifically told not to select a similar-looking product merely to complete the screen. A delay recorded accurately was less harmful than a fast transaction that silently changed the stock balance.||Looking beyond the first week~After a month, management reviewed whether staff still followed the scanning sequence during busy periods. Most did, but temporary workers had received only a brief verbal introduction. The company created a short practice order for every new starter, including those hired for a single shift. Experienced workers could demonstrate the task quickly, while beginners received more support. The standard stayed the same without assuming that everybody needed exactly the same amount of teaching.',
'The difference between access and ability~The larger display helped my colleague read the screen, but it did not teach her the new process. She still completed the same practice order as everyone else. This distinction mattered: an adjustment removed an unnecessary obstacle while leaving the learning requirement intact. She later noticed that the warning color was hard to distinguish in bright sunlight. The software team added an icon so users did not have to depend on color alone.||What happened after a connection failure~During a real order, the device lost contact with the network. I used the approved temporary record and marked the order as awaiting synchronization. When the connection returned, the supervisor checked that no item had been entered twice. It took longer than pretending nothing had happened, but the final stock record was reliable. I wrote down where the failure occurred so the technical team could look for a pattern rather than investigate each incident as an isolated accident.')

extend(23,
'A stop in the wrong place~Employers initially counted staff who finished after ten, assuming all of them could reach the same bus stop. A site visit showed that workers from one factory had to walk through an unlit service road to get there. The operator moved the stop closer to a shared exit where lighting and pedestrian access were better. This did not change the departure time, but it made the service a realistic choice for more potential passengers.||Keeping the information current~The printed timetable described the trial\'s end date and gave a contact for feedback. When the council extended the trial, old posters remained in several staff rooms. Some workers stopped using the bus because they believed it had ended. Employers then nominated one person at each site to replace notices. Reliable communication was part of running the service: a bus that existed but was believed to have stopped could not attract the demand its funding depended on.',
'When a connection cannot be guaranteed~The operator explained that the two buses were run under separate contracts. One driver could not always wait for the other without delaying later passengers. This helped me understand the constraint, although it did not solve my journey. I asked whether the published journey planner could show a warning about the short connection. A suggested route should not look equally reliable when one option leaves only a few minutes and another provides a comfortable margin.||An alternative with a different cost~On days when I had an important appointment, I took an earlier service and spent the extra time in the public library near my destination. This reduced the risk of arriving late without paying for a taxi. It was not a solution available every day because I sometimes worked until the later departure. In my feedback, I therefore separated a useful personal workaround from a change that would make the transport system more dependable for all shift workers.')

extend(24,
'Rules that people can apply~The library avoided a vague instruction simply telling visitors to behave appropriately. Signs gave examples: use headphones downstairs, take phone calls outside and keep conversation out of the upstairs room. Staff could then refer to the same expectations consistently. A visitor who misunderstood a sign received an explanation before any stronger action. The intention was to make different activities compatible, not to make first-time users feel that they had entered a place full of unwritten rules.||A wider picture of demand~Visitor counts showed that the study room was especially busy before local examinations. During other periods, seats were usually available. Rather than permanently removing space from children\'s activities, the library tested temporary extra study tables during those weeks. It published the dates early so regular groups could plan around the change. Seasonal information made a targeted response possible and prevented a short period of high demand from being mistaken for the same pressure throughout the year.',
'A request that became specific~My first suggestion had simply been that the library needed more space. A librarian asked when I had failed to find a seat and how long I usually stayed. After recording three visits, I realized that the problem was concentrated between three and five on Tuesdays. Those details were more useful than my general impression. They also showed that arriving half an hour later sometimes solved the problem without requiring the library to make an immediate change.||What a quiet room cannot provide~The room improved my concentration, but it did not decide what I should study or explain material I did not understand. I still needed a manageable task and a way to check my answers. I began arriving with one clear reading goal and a short list of questions. The physical environment removed distractions; the study plan gave that uninterrupted time a purpose. Treating either one as sufficient on its own would have exaggerated what the library could do for my learning.')

extend(25,
'The value of a written estimate~The estimate separated the inspection, replacement pump and labor. It also explained what would happen if the repairer found a second fault after opening the machine. The family would receive a new quotation before further work began. They photographed the model label and kept the report with the receipt. These documents would be useful if the same fault returned, although they did not turn the repair into a guarantee against every future mechanical problem.||Considering inconvenience as well as price~A replacement machine could not be delivered for twelve days, while the pump was available locally. During the wait, the family would have needed a laundrette or help from relatives. They included that inconvenience in their decision rather than comparing purchase prices alone. Someone planning to move soon might place a different value on repairing a heavy appliance. The example illustrates why a repair decision depends on condition, use and circumstances, not simply the age printed on the machine.',
'A lesson in choosing an example~The volunteer showed me a second lamp whose plastic casing had become brittle. That lamp was not a good candidate for the same repair because opening it could cause further damage. Seeing two different cases prevented me from concluding that every flickering lamp needed a new switch. A symptom can have several causes, and a successful repair provides evidence about the item examined, not a universal diagnosis for all items with a similar problem.||Recording what was actually done~Before leaving, I wrote down the part replaced and the advice I had received. The event organizer gave me contact details for professional services if the problem returned. The lamp worked during the check, but volunteers did not claim to have tested it under every possible condition. I appreciated the clear boundary. A confident-sounding promise would have been less useful than knowing what had been inspected, what remained uncertain and whom to contact if I noticed another fault.')

extend(26,
'A decision log rather than a transcript~The shared board included a decision log with an owner and review date. It did not contain every sentence spoken during meetings. When a decision depended on a supplier\'s response, that condition was written beside it. Without this detail, colleagues reading the board later might act as if the decision were final. The team learned that shortening meetings required improving the information around them, not simply setting a timer and ending every discussion when it rang.||Making room for disagreement~At the end of important discussions, the chair asked whether anyone saw a risk that had not been recorded. This was more focused than asking whether everybody was happy. Staff could disagree with a proposal without having to reject the whole project. One warning about customer-support capacity led to a smaller initial launch. The longer conversation saved work later, reminding the team that the purpose of a shorter-meeting policy was better use of time rather than shorter meetings at any cost.',
'A question that changed the quotation~During follow-up, I asked whether technical support was available in our time zone. The supplier\'s original answer had described total support hours without stating when they occurred. Some of those hours fell during our night. We agreed on a response arrangement for urgent problems and recorded it separately from ordinary enquiries. The equipment itself had not changed, but the practical value of the offer was now much clearer.||Using the record later~Two weeks after delivery, an accessory was missing. I checked the meeting summary and the final order before contacting the supplier. The summary showed that the accessory had been discussed, while the order showed that it had not actually been included. I could ask for a new quotation, but could not fairly claim that the supplier had failed to deliver an ordered item. Keeping suggestions separate from confirmed commitments protected both sides from a disagreement based only on memory.')

extend(27,
'Turning advice into a usable system~The class prepared labels showing when a container had been opened and when leftovers had been stored. These did not replace the original manufacturer\'s instructions. Instead, they helped households remember events that were otherwise easy to forget. Participants also discussed where to place older items in the fridge so they remained visible. A good intention to waste less food was more likely to become action when storage made the next decision obvious.||Avoiding an unsafe conclusion~One participant asked whether reducing waste meant keeping everything until it looked spoiled. The tutor explained that waste reduction should begin with purchasing, portioning and correct storage, not ignoring safety advice. Some hazards are not visible. The group therefore practised reading the whole instruction, including what to do after opening. They compared two similar-looking products with different requirements, learning that a familiar category name does not remove the need to check the actual label on the item being used.',
'A cost that was easy to miss~Buying a larger pack seemed economical when I compared the price per gram. However, that calculation assumed we would eat all of it. Once I included the portion thrown away, the smaller pack sometimes cost less per meal actually eaten. I did not apply this conclusion to every product: rice could be stored for much longer than fresh salad. The useful question was how the purchase matched our habits, not whether large packs were always good or always wasteful.||Making the record sustainable~At first I tried weighing every discarded item, which made the task slow and irritating. I changed to a simple count of portions with a brief reason, such as forgotten, bought too much or disliked. The measurements became less precise, but I kept recording consistently. For our household decision, a reliable pattern was more useful than detailed measurements abandoned after three days. I noted the change in method so I would not compare the two sets of figures as if they were identical.')

extend(28,
'A charge with a different name~The booking page listed a cleaning charge below the nightly rate. Because it applied once per stay, its effect was larger for a single night than for a week. The traveller recalculated the total for the actual dates rather than multiplying the advertised nightly amount and stopping there. She also checked whether the local tax was collected online or on arrival, so the final payment request would not be mistaken for an unexpected change in price.||Planning for uncertainty~The key-code arrangement depended on a working phone and a message sent before reception closed. The traveller saved the host\'s contact number separately and downloaded the arrival instructions while connected to the internet. These small preparations did not guarantee a problem-free journey, but they reduced dependence on one device or connection at the moment of arrival. She also told the host when her estimated arrival changed, recognizing that a booking system might not receive updates from the railway company automatically.',
'Distinguishing a request from permission~A guest once asked to host a small birthday gathering in the apartment. The maximum overnight occupancy was four, but that limit did not define how many visitors could attend an event. We explained the separate rule against parties before accepting the booking. Stating the restriction early gave the guest a chance to choose a more suitable venue. Waiting until arrival would have created an avoidable dispute and disturbed the neighbors who share the building.||A departure problem~When a guest accidentally took the keys home, we asked them to contact us immediately rather than wait until they could post them. The next arrival needed access that afternoon. We arranged a spare set and then agreed how the original keys would be returned. Our message explained the actual costs involved instead of assuming every lost-key situation required the same charge. Clear communication allowed the problem to be solved without confusing an honest mistake with an intention to ignore the rules.')

extend(29,
'Feedback on a productive mistake~In one conversation, the learner used a familiar phrase in a situation where it sounded too direct. Her partner explained the effect on the listener and offered a more appropriate alternative. The original phrase was not grammatically wrong, so a spelling or grammar score would not have revealed the difficulty. She recorded both situations and practised deciding which expression fitted each one. Knowing a phrase included understanding its social use, not only recalling its dictionary meaning.||Setting a stopping rule~The learner sometimes continued reviewing until she could answer every item correctly in one sitting. This left little time for listening and writing. She introduced an eight-minute review limit and allowed unfinished items to remain due. On difficult days, she reduced new vocabulary instead of extending the entire session. The goal was a balanced routine that could continue for months. A perfect short-term review score was not worth sacrificing the other skills required for actual communication.',
'A disagreement about feedback~Sometimes my partner and I preferred different expressions. Rather than deciding that one must be wrong, we asked whether the difference concerned meaning, formality or personal style. We checked reliable examples when the issue mattered. This stopped every alternative wording from becoming an unnecessary correction. For examination preparation, we concentrated on whether the message was clear and appropriate instead of trying to make both speakers sound identical.||Building an unfamiliar task~At the end of the month, we each wrote a new scenario for the other person. Mine involved changing a booking after a train cancellation; my partner\'s involved explaining a problem with a shared appliance. We used familiar language in situations we had not rehearsed exactly. After recording the conversation, we identified one successful repair of a misunderstanding and one point that still needed work. The task gave us evidence of flexibility that repeated vocabulary quizzes had not supplied.')

extend(30,
'A disagreement about membership~Some experienced residents wanted unrestricted access immediately, arguing that they had used similar tools for years. The organizers explained that induction also covered the building, emergency arrangements and shared responsibilities. Familiarity with a tool did not establish knowledge of this particular workshop. To avoid wasting experienced members\' time, the demonstration could be shorter when competence was evident, but the site-specific requirements remained. The policy separated recognition of prior skill from exemption from all procedures.||What the first report would contain~The proposed quarterly report included opening hours, completed inductions, repairs attempted and repairs completed. Unsuccessful attempts were included because they could reveal missing equipment or a need for specialist advice. The organizers also planned to record why prospective members decided not to join. A high number of repaired chairs would be encouraging, but it would not answer every question about accessibility, affordability or the range of people the workshop was intended to serve.',
'A budget with alternatives~The application distinguished essential purchases from items that could wait. Safe electrical installation and accessible entry were required before opening; a second set of specialist tools could be added later if demand justified it. This made it possible for the funder to understand how a smaller award would change the project. A budget that treated every item as equally urgent would have provided less useful information for a decision under limited resources.||A plan for unexpected costs~The group included a modest contingency and stated what approval would be needed before using it. If alterations cost more than expected, the organizers would revise the equipment schedule rather than quietly divert restricted funds. They also named a person responsible for keeping invoices and updating the budget. This role was not glamorous, but it made the promised accountability practical. Community enthusiasm could start the project; reliable records and realistic commitments would help it continue after the opening event.')
